<title>RUSSIA: AGROKHLEB-Russian sunseed yields down in poor crop.</title>
<headline>AGROKHLEB-Russian sunseed yields down in poor crop.</headline>
<text>
<p>The following is a direct contribution from the Agrokhleb Report, published by SovEcon Ltd, an independent research organisation specialising in agricultural production and trade in the former Soviet Union.</p>
<p>RUSSIAN SUNSEED YIELDS IN SLIDE AS POOR HARVEST PROGRESSES</p>
<p>MOSCOW, Oct 29 - By October 21, the harvested sunflower seed area had increased to 2.2 million hectares (67 percent of total plantings), below last year's 2.8 million hectares.</p>
<p>Though the pace of the harvest has accelerated, it is lagging in most areas behind normal levels, particularly in the Rostov region and in western Siberia.</p>
<p>As of October 21, the average yield achieved from the harvested area had declined to 0.87 tonnes per hectare, which is 20 percent below last year's level (Table 1).</p>
<p>It is expected that Russia's final average sunseed yield may decline to 0.8 tonnes per hectare, which will be the lowest this decade.</p>
<p>At this stage, SovEcon's forecast for Russia's total sunseed output remains virtually unchanged at about 3.1 million tonnes, or 1.1 million tonnes below last year's record production.</p>
<p>The sharpest decline is expected in the Northern Caucasus, particularly in Rostov, in western Siberia and in Saratov, in the Povolzhsk region. Output this year is forecast to increase only in the Urals (Table 2).</p>
